{-
   `Once` - run this exactly once, and make everyone else wait for it.

   The smallest thing that needs parking and the one that most obviously does: lazy initialization
   without it is either a lock around every read, or a race in which two threads both build the
   thing. What `Once` promises is that the initializer runs one time, that every caller returns only
   after it has finished, and that what it wrote is visible to all of them.

   § Three states

   `0` nobody has started, `1` somebody is running it, `2` it is done. The fast path is a single
   **acquire load** answering `2`, which is one instruction and no branch worth predicting - and it
   has to be acquire rather than relaxed, because what the caller is about to read is whatever the
   initializer wrote. That edge is the whole point: a relaxed load here would let a caller see "done"
   and then read uninitialized storage, which is the exact bug lazy initialization is famous for.

   The runner publishes with a release store and wakes everybody, because they are all waiting for
   the same event and all of them can proceed.
-}
import Native
import Atomic
import Native.Thread.Linux

let notStarted = 0 :: U32
let running = 1 :: U32
let finished = 2 :: U32

pub data Once {state: Atomic(U32)}

pub fn once() -> Once = Once {state: atomic(notStarted)}

-- Whether it has finished. The acquire is not decoration: a caller that reads this and then reads
-- what the initializer wrote needs the edge, and one that only wants a diagnostic pays nothing for
-- it on this architecture.
pub fn hasRun(self: Once) -> Bool = self.state.load(LoadAcquire) == finished

{-
   `callOnce(self, body)` - run `body` if nobody has, and wait if somebody is.

   Three paths and each of them is short. The first caller takes the state from `notStarted` to
   `running`, runs the body, publishes `finished` and wakes whoever gathered. A later caller sees
   `finished` on the fast load and returns. A caller that arrives while the body is running parks on
   the word until it changes, then re-reads - which is a loop rather than a single wait because a
   park may return for reasons that have nothing to do with this.

   **Nothing is unwound if the body fails.** Yana has no unwinding, so a body that stops the process
   stops it; a body that returns is a body that ran. There is deliberately no "poisoned" state to
   observe, because there is no failure for it to record.

   A higher-order function rather than a lens, because a lens always continues into the block below
   it and this must **not** - the whole point is that the second caller does not run the body. That
   is a skipping lens, which needs a `Try` instance to say where the skip goes, and a body-taking
   function says the same thing with nothing to explain.
-}
pub fn callOnce(self: Once, body: () -> {}) -> {}:
    if self.state.load(LoadAcquire) == finished: return

    match self.state.compareExchange(notStarted, running, UpdateAcquire):
        Exchanged -> runAndPublish(self, body)
        Unchanged(->_) -> awaitFinished(self)

fn runAndPublish(self: Once, body: () -> {}) -> {}:
    body()

    -- Release, so that everything the body wrote is visible to every caller that observes this - and
    -- the wake comes after, because a waiter that starts before the store would find `running` and
    -- park again.
    self.state.store(finished, StoreRelease)
    futexWake(self.state, 0x7FFFFFFF)

fn awaitFinished(self: Once) -> {}:
    while True:
        let observed = self.state.load(LoadAcquire)
        if observed == finished: break

        futexWait(self.state, observed)

